cho \\\"${line}\\\" >> \\\"${GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY}\\\"\\n00391|           if [[ \\\"${total}\\\" -ne \\\"${SLICE}\\\" ]]; then\\n00392|             echo \\\"::error::only ${total} of ${SLICE} cases were graded; a shard did not finish\\\" >&2\\n00393|             exit 1\\n00394|           fi\\n\\n(End of file - total 394 lines)\\n</file>\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794762838837/src/coding/structural_composition.rs and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_f81b7f68c31021b5\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794762838837/src/coding/structural_composition.rs\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_f81b7f68c31021b5\",\"content\":\"Error: File not found: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794762838837/src/coding/structural_composition.rs\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me take the next step to handle that for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_72628516590259e4\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"glob\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"**/structural_composition.rs\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_72628516590259e4\",\"content\":\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1794762838837/rust/tests/unit/coding_discovery/structural_composition.rs\"},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"You are an AI issue solver using @link-assistant/agent. Prefer minimal local CPU, memory, and disk use during development.\\nGeneral guidelines.\\n   - When you execute commands and the output becomes large, save the logs to files for easier review.\\n   - When running commands, avoid setting a timeout yourself. Let them run as long as needed.\\n   - When running sudo commands, especially package installations, run them in the background to avoid timeout issues.\\n   - When CI is failing, download the logs locally and investigate them carefully.\\n   - When a code or log file has more than 1500 lines, read it in chunks of 1500 lines.\\n   - When diagnosing a complex problem, prefer targeted tracing and enable verbose output only where useful.\\n   - When you create debug, test, or example scripts while fixing an issue, keep them in ./examples and/or ./experiments so you can reuse them later.\\n   - When you test assumptions, keep experiment scripts in ./experiments.\\n   - When an experiment demonstrates a real-world use case of the software, add it to ./examples.\\nInitial research.\\n   - When you start, outline the work and track requirements and verification in your plan.\\n   - When you read issue, read all details and comments thoroughly.\\n   - When you see screenshots or images in issue descriptions, pull request descriptions, comments, or discussions, download the image to a local file first, then use the Read tool to view and analyze it. Before reading downloaded images with the Read tool, verify that the file is a valid image rather than HTML by using a CLI tool such as the 'file' command. When the file command shows \\\"HTML\\\", \\\"text\\\", or \\\"ASCII text\\\", the download failed, so do not call Read on that file. When images are from GitHub issues or PRs, such as URLs containing \\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\", use: curl -L -H \\\"Authorization: token $(gh auth token)\\\" -o <filename> \\\"<url>\\\"\\n   - When you need issue details, use gh issue view https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1574.\\n   - When you need related code, use gh search code --owner link-assistant [keywords].\\n   - When you need repo context, read files in your working directory.\\n   - When you study related work, study the most recent related pull requests.\\n   - When the issue is not defined clearly enough, write a comment with clarifying questions.\\n   - When accessing GitHub Gists, use gh gist view command instead of direct URL fetching.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug, find the actual root cause first and run as many experiments as needed.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug and the code does not have enough tracing or logs, add them and keep them in the code with the default state switched off.\\n   - When you need comments on a pull request, note that GitHub has three different comment types with different API endpoints:\\n      1. PR review comments (inline code comments): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/1575/comments --paginate\\n      2. PR conversation comments (general discussion): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1575/comments --paginate\\n      3. PR reviews (approve/request changes): gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/pulls/1575/reviews --paginate\\n      Note: The command \\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\" only returns conversation comments and misses review comments.\\n   - When you need the latest comments on the issue, use gh api repos/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/1574/comments --paginate.\\nSolution development and testing.\\n   - When coding, commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request branch so interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n   - When implementing changes, prefer drafting code for all requirements before starting verification, then use focused checks and existing CI/CD coverage.\\n   - When validating changes, prefer a single relevant test or small focused checks; check whether the command triggers a full source build before running it.\\n   - When CI/CD already covers full test suites or CPU-intensive builds, prefer leaving those to CI/CD and checking results for the current commit; avoid duplicating them locally unless user requirements or uncovered failures call for it.\\n   - When CI/CD coverage is missing and adding it was not requested, suggest https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md; let the repository owner decide whether to add it, and meanwhile complete required validation with available resources.\\n   - When writing tests, prefer small cases with mocks and the existing integration framework.\\n   - When you test solution draft, include automated checks in pr.\\n   - When you write or modify tests, consider setting reasonable timeouts at test, suite, and CI job levels so failures surface quickly instead of hanging.\\n   - When you see repeated test timeout patterns in CI, investigate the root cause rather than increasing timeouts.\\n   - When the issue is unclear, write a comment on the issue with questions.\\n   - When you encounter any problems that you are unable to solve yourself, write a comment to the pull request asking for help.\\n   - When you need human help, use gh pr comment 1575 --body \\\"your message\\\" to comment on existing PR.\\nReproducible testing.\\n   - When fixing a bug, prefer a minimal regression test that fails before the fix and passes afterward.\\n   - When encountering UI bugs, capture a screenshot showing the problem state, then create a visual regression test or manual verification screenshot after the fix.\\n   - When submitting a fix, include in the PR description: (1) how to reproduce the issue, (2) the automated test that verifies the fix, (3) before/after screenshots for UI issues.\\n   - When a bug fix does not have a reproducing test, treat the fix as incomplete because regressions can occur later without notice.\\nPreparing pull request.\\n   - When you code, follow contributing guidelines.\\n   - When you commit, write clear message.\\n   - When you need examples of style, use gh pr list --repo link-assistant/formal-ai --state merged --search [keywords].\\n   - When you open pr, describe solution draft and include tests.\\n   - When there is a package with version and GitHub Actions workflows for automatic release, update the version in your pull request to prepare for next release.\\n   - When you update existing pr 1575, use gh pr edit to modify title and description.\\n   - When you finalize the pull request:\\n      check that the pull request title and description are updated (the PR may start with 